# Scratch — screenshots Briggsy drops for review, agent debris. Never committed.
temp/
temp-*

# War-room scratch, written into draft-kit/ during a live draft or mock and read by
# draft_engine.py from its cwd. Per-draft state, worthless the moment the draft ends.
draft-kit/picks.json
draft-kit/new_picks.json
draft-kit/slot_names.json

# Discarded Imagen rolls + superseded avatar variants. Kept on disk as the visual
# record of how the logos got made; too heavy (30MB of 2-3MB PNGs) to carry in history.
# The two keepers — logo/deez-nuts/ and logo/hunter-maker/ — ARE tracked.
logo/archive/

# Watcher state is per-machine runtime, not source. last_seen.json is the diff baseline (it
# changes every hour, on every machine, and means nothing to anyone else) and DRAFT_ALERTS.md is
# local output Briggsy reads. Committing either would rewrite the repo hourly for zero value --
# same reasoning as the inbox below. .gitkeep so a clean clone still has somewhere to write.
newsletter/data/state/*
!newsletter/data/state/.gitkeep

# Mule cargo is a CACHE, not source: the hourly job overwrites every file in inbox/
# (the two RSS feeds alone churn ~1.2MB an hour) and the newsletter build drains it
# into data/archive/. Committing either would rewrite the repo every hour for zero value.
# Both directories keep a .gitkeep so a clean clone still has somewhere to land cargo.
newsletter/data/inbox/*
!newsletter/data/inbox/.gitkeep
newsletter/data/archive/*
!newsletter/data/archive/.gitkeep

# Back issues, ignored 2026-08-08 by Briggsy's call, and the argument is the two lines above:
# the CARGO each edition was built from is already ignored "for zero value", so committing the
# edition while ignoring its own inputs was backwards. Each is 20,494 bytes of standalone HTML
# with the whole stylesheet inlined -- ~7.5MB a year of near-duplicate markup nobody reopens,
# plus a commit every morning on the branch this project uses as its session log.
# THE CURRENT EDITION STAYS TRACKED at newsletter/family-feud-newsletter.html, the way every
# generated surface in draft-kit/ is tracked. Only the pile is ignored, not the paper.
# Consequence, accepted deliberately: edition numbering is derived from the back issues on disk
# (build_newsletter.edition_number), so a clean clone restarts at #1. Briggsy does not want a
# number nobody reads to survive a laptop death, and the alternative was a tracked counter --
# a hand-maintained duplicate, which is the class this rebuild exists to kill.
newsletter/archive/*
!newsletter/archive/.gitkeep

# nflverse weekly stats are a CACHE, not source: ~1.6MB per season, six seasons on disk, and
# every byte is re-downloadable from a versioned GitHub release. `draft-kit/cache/` itself is
# NOT ignored -- sleeper_players.json.gz lives there and IS committed, because U14's frozen ids
# are only meaningful against the exact dump they were resolved from.
# The oracle these feed is committed instead, at tests/fixtures/player_stats_2024_reg.csv.gz
# (one real season, 324KB gzipped), so the scoring tests survive a clean clone.
draft-kit/cache/player_stats_*.csv
# Same asset, current release name. nflverse's `player_stats` release is FROZEN at 2024; the live
# one is `stats_player`, which publishes 1999-2025 and folds kicking into the weekly file. Both
# stems are ignored because both are pure cache -- see SOURCES in scripts/build_curves.py.
draft-kit/cache/stats_player_week_*.csv
# Play-by-play is the same deal, an order of magnitude larger (~19MB/season
# gzipped). It is the route to the 2025 season and the long-TD bonuses; see
# the docstring in scripts/build_curves.py before fetching it again.
draft-kit/cache/play_by_play_*.csv.gz

# Historical preseason ADP, one small file per season, for scripts/realized_value.py. Unlike the
# consensus and current-ADP caches these are IMMUTABLE -- 2015's ADP will never change again --
# so the usual "a committed copy would look current when it is stale" argument does not apply.
# They are ignored for a different reason: nothing on draft morning reads them, and the test
# suite runs on fixtures rather than on these, so a clean clone loses nothing. `--refetch` or a
# plain run rebuilds them in a couple of minutes.
draft-kit/cache/adp_history/

# Opponent scouting cargo -- one file per league member, every league/draft/pick/roster they have.
# Pure cache: re-pullable with `scout_opponents.py --fetch`, and it grows every season a member
# plays. The FINDINGS are committed instead, at docs/opponents.md, which is the surface anybody
# should be reading -- a committed cache would go stale silently while still looking scouted.
draft-kit/cache/opponents/

# The expert consensus is the one input here that is SUPPOSED to be stale-checked rather than
# pinned -- its whole value is being newer than the board. Committing it would put a daily churning
# 240KB blob in git and, worse, make a clean clone look like it had a current consensus when it had
# whatever was current the day someone committed. Re-fetch with `consensus.py --refresh`.
draft-kit/cache/fp_ecr.csv.gz
# Market ADP, same reasoning: its whole value is being current, and a committed copy would make a
# clean clone look priced when it was priced on whatever day someone committed. `market.py --refresh`.
draft-kit/cache/ffc_adp.json.gz
# player_ids.csv.gz is the OPPOSITE and IS committed, alongside sleeper_players.json.gz: it is the
# only bridge from the board's frozen sleeperId to FantasyPros (their own cross-id columns are the
# literal string "NA" on every row), it barely moves, and on draft morning it must not need a
# network call to exist.

# U6: the pre-emit backup the generator restores from when a replace fails mid-set. Per-machine
# runtime, rebuilt on every build; build_manifest.json IS tracked because it is the proof the
# surfaces were generated and not hand-edited.
draft-kit/.last_good/

# Playwright MCP scratch output (snapshots, console logs, screenshots) from browser verification.
.playwright-mcp/
